# The four copy-bearing messages of prefs.rs, byte-for-byte. These are the
# product's densest privacy claims; every one carries its own limit in the
# same sentence, and the claims manifest pins their exact content. Editing
# one is a review-gated event ON PURPOSE: regenerate the manifest in the
# same change, or the build fails naming the message.

prefs-dns-system-description = The default. Your machine keeps using whatever it uses now: your VPN's resolver if you have one, otherwise your internet provider, who can log and sell what you look up. The lookup is not encrypted, so a network can also strip the key that hides the site name inside the connection and put it back in the open. This is the only setting that works on public WiFi that asks you to log in.

prefs-dns-quad9-description = Encrypted, and sent to Quad9, a Swiss nonprofit that also blocks known malicious domains. Quad9 sees every domain you look up instead of your network. It also protects the key that hides the site name inside the connection, which a network can strip over unencrypted DNS. It overrides a VPN's resolver and never falls back to the network's DNS, so public WiFi sign-in pages will not load until you switch back to System and restart.

prefs-tunnel-off-description = The default. Your browsing goes straight out, the same as any other browser.

prefs-tunnel-imported-description = Sends only this browser's traffic through the WireGuard server you imported, not your other apps and not the rest of the system. You picked that server, and it can see your traffic, so this is not an anonymity feature. If the tunnel goes down, pages fail to load. PATANYX never falls back to a direct connection. Switching this on or off takes effect the next time you start the browser; Apply and restart, in the Private Tunnel panel, does that for you and reopens your tabs.


